(i)
- In most angiosperms the primary endosperm cell divides first and forms the triploid endosperm tissue, and the zygote begins to divide only after a certain amount of endosperm has formed. Embryo development follows endosperm development because this is an **adaptation that assures nutrition** to the developing embryo.
- In a mature **albuminous** seed (for example wheat, maize, castor), part of the endosperm persists and acts as the **food store that nourishes the embryo** when the seed germinates.

(ii) The stages of embryo development in a dicot, drawn in sequence and labelled:
- **Zygote**, at the micropylar end of the embryo sac;
- the zygote divides to form the **proembryo** and then the **globular embryo**, attached to the embryo sac wall by a chain of cells, the **suspensor**;
- the **heart-shaped embryo**, in which the two cotyledons begin to grow out;
- the **mature embryo**, with an embryonal axis carrying the **radicle** (root tip) at one end and the **plumule** (stem tip) at the other, the two **cotyledons** attached to the axis, and the suspensor still at the radicle end.

The marking scheme credits any six correct labels from zygote, globular embryo, heart-shaped embryo, mature embryo, suspensor, radicle, plumule and cotyledon.
